WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.550
Film Courage: What about the opposite? What
if someone says “I’m never going to get

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:06.930
notes like that. I’m going to have it [the
script] packed with conflict. And it’s actually

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:09.530
too much. It’s not subtle, it’s not under
the surface.

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:14.120
I’m just thinking of HBO’s SHARP OBJECTS,
I watched the first episode last night. And

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:20.200
you can see seeds planted. The conflict - some
of it’s obvious, some of it’s not and

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.759
you’re wondering “Hhmmmm? There was a
look at the end with this one character. I

00:00:23.759 --> 00:00:27.449
think there’s going to be conflict.” It
hints at it and then it keeps you hooked in

00:00:27.449 --> 00:00:32.080
because you’re like “I want to see someone
go down here.” But it doesn’t yet.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:39.000
Erik Bork: It’s not that you have to hit
people over the head with like constant screaming

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.940
matches or fights or anything like that. To
me there has to be a central problem and a

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:50.150
central character faced with that problem
that the audience buys into early on and says

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:56.590
“Okay, I care about this problem.” I want
to see this problem get solved for this person.

00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:03.190
Once you have them feeling that way then you
can play out the resolution of that problem

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:08.350
over a lengthy period of time. You don’t
have to get to it in a really sledge hammer

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:13.780
kind of manner. But the problem has to be
big enough to begin with. It has to be grabby

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:18.659
enough to begin with. But then how they involve
dealing with the problem and how it evolves

00:01:18.659 --> 00:01:23.479
and then if it’s a mystery how the cards
get turned over and how the clues get revealed

00:01:23.479 --> 00:01:27.759
and the leads get followed up on, that’s
part of the fun for the audience is that that

00:01:27.759 --> 00:01:32.330
stuff takes time. You just don’t want to
have long periods where none of that is happening

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:36.639
at all and things just seem kind of fine.
That’s what you want to avoid. Things are

00:01:36.639 --> 00:01:43.170
never fine in a story. There’s always that
simmering problem overshadowing everything

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:48.039
in a commercial story where the audience is
going “I want to see this resolved.” The

00:01:48.039 --> 00:01:52.790
reason I am binging on this is I want to see
this problem or problems get resolved. What

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:57.490
they really want to see is how the problem
builds and complicates and twists and turns

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:02.329
and evolves and then later get resolved because
you know once it gets resolved it’s kind

00:02:02.329 --> 00:02:08.030
of over. So if you’re binging on 12 episodes
or whatever it is, you probably know no real

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:12.500
resolution is going to happen until the end
of the 12 but (if then) but on the way how

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:16.590
it’s evolving is the thing that is fun for
us to watch as the audience.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:20.970
Film Courage: Have you given notes to someone
“Hey tone it down, this is too much right

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:26.599
here. It’s too explosive and it’s almost
like beating the reader over the head a little

00:02:26.599 --> 00:02:27.599
bit?”

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:30.411
Erik: I don’t think that happens very much
unless what they’re doing doesn’t feel

00:02:30.411 --> 00:02:34.400
believable to me. That’s sort of the B in
P R O B L E M [the concept of Erik’s book]

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.299
is it believable and that’s kind of a bigger,
more common issue than people might think

00:02:39.299 --> 00:02:42.470
where readers are reading their stuff and
going “I don’t believe this character

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:47.650
would do or say this.” Or maybe it feels
contrived in some way. It feels like the writer

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:52.860
is trying to force something that isn’t
organically coming out of the lives and desires

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:58.000
and emotions and personalities of the characters.
So to some extent the characters do have to

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.370
drive the story. Everything they’re doing
has to feel like it could and would really

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:09.060
happen. They could or would really say those
things in those moments. So it’s not that

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:12.980
I’m saying “Oh, there’s too much conflict.
You’re trying to make the conflict too big”

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:18.420
per se unless in doing that it’s straining
believability.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:22.900
Because even though stories have usually something
exaggerated about them or even sometimes a

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:26.909
fantastical element that they are based on
like there’s a zombie apocalypse or there

00:03:26.909 --> 00:03:32.349
is vampires in high school or whatever, even
thought they have that kind of stuff sometimes

00:03:32.349 --> 00:03:36.829
and even in a comedy you’re exaggerating
characters a little bit for comedy, I believe

00:03:36.829 --> 00:03:41.689
there has to be this basis of reality. That
we’re seeing human beings that we can accept

00:03:41.689 --> 00:03:46.870
seem real doing or saying what we think they
would really do or say in those situations

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:53.569
and a lot of times in a lot of scripts there
will be moments that seem that way. That feel

00:03:53.569 --> 00:04:00.680
over the top, unbelievable, reacting in ways
that seem forced for the sake of more conflict

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:07.700
or comedy or entertainment value or more action.
So that’s where I feel that something needs

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:12.319
to be toned down so it feels like it’s coming
from a real place.

00:04:12.319 --> 00:04:17.940
When in doubt I say always go for the real.
I heard someone say this once (a professional

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:23.280
writer at a panel) and it’s so true, you
hope that your concept is compelling enough,

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:26.919
people are going to care, there is something
entertaining at it’s core but when you start

00:04:26.919 --> 00:04:32.020
plotting it out, I always say “Start with
what’s real.” At every moment in the story,

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:36.870
what would every character be thinking, feeling,
doing at this point in the situation, given

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:42.150
their situation. How do you make them do things
that feel totally believable and just totally

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:48.931
real. Audiences love it when it feels real
but still is entertaining and compelling and

00:04:48.931 --> 00:04:54.020
all those other things. And achieving that
level of realness that feels really authentic

00:04:54.020 --> 00:05:00.400
is difficult but it’s really special when
a writer is able to just write with authenticity

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:06.910
where I absolutely suspend disbelief, I’m
absolutely happy, I believe these people are

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:11.050
real, I forget that I’m watching something
fictional, I forget that I’m watching something

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:14.669
fictional. So when you can do that in the
context of having these other elements in

00:05:14.669 --> 00:05:16.800
a story, I think you are in really good shape.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:21.100
Film Courage: Can you think about a film in
particular that was one of the first that

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:22.510
did that for you?

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:31.349
Erik: Well I think most great and really successful
movies and TV shows do have that basis of

00:05:31.349 --> 00:05:35.410
reality which is why they work. If they didn’t
have it, I don’t think they’d be successful.

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:40.210
So I think you see that in most successful
things so there is this sense of I believe

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:43.789
these people and what they’re doing, I believe
the reality of the situation that the writer

00:05:43.789 --> 00:05:48.849
has set up. Having said that there are certain
shows, certain movies that I can think of

00:05:48.849 --> 00:05:51.970
(maybe not the first that did it) but that
feel like they did it to a really extreme

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:59.740
level like THE WIRE on HBO (that series) to
me made every other cop show that came before

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:06.770
seem almost like cheesy or fake in comparison
because it felt so real and when you can achieve

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:14.580
that level of realness, audiences, critics,
agents, producers, they all respond to that.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:19.020
If you have all these other elements of story
in place, that level of realness can really

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:22.290
make you stuff special (so that’s one example).

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:28.460
THE SOPRANOS was also a show that even though
it was not trying to be as real as THE WIRE,

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:32.500
when I would watch THE SOPRANOS I would just
kind of buy it for some reason. I don’t

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:38.280
feel like it was a show that I’m watching
actors and writers and directors. I feel like

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:43.051
I’m watching these real people that are
living in this subculture at its best. Which

00:06:43.051 --> 00:06:48.099
I think is the reason why shows like THE SOPRANOS
and THE WIRE are voted like the best written

00:06:48.099 --> 00:06:52.039
shows of all time because they obviously did
that for a lot of people.

00:06:52.039 --> 00:06:58.330
Film Courage: Even Forrest Gump, I can think
that this is a real person that if you go

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:04.849
on Wikipedia…oh…there he is. And yes he
beat this person in ping-pong and it’s believable

00:07:04.849 --> 00:07:12.099
enough as wild as a ride as his life was that
he could be a real person. There is just something

00:07:12.099 --> 00:07:17.590
about the writing and drawing you into this
sort of innocent world.

00:07:17.590 --> 00:07:23.479
Erik: Yeah, I think you’re right. I think
it’s an opposite example of a very fanciful

00:07:23.479 --> 00:07:30.300
story but some how it convinces the audience
for those 2 to 3 hours that this is a real

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:33.732
thing. And nothing is making him go “Okay,
it’s not real anymore. I don’t get it.”

00:07:33.732 --> 00:07:37.260
I mean there are people who don’t like the
movie but I don’t think there is much where

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:41.780
people check out because it doesn’t seem
like it could be plausible.

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:46.500
The characters once you establish who they
are, they behave in ways that make sense based

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:50.630
on who they are, their psychology and what
their backstories are and what their desires

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:56.189
and situations are and that’s where the
writer (Erik Roth in this case) is going for

00:07:56.189 --> 00:08:03.710
the real thing. The situation, the people
and saying what is real to have happen next.

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:08.189
Film Courage: And plus he’s throwing in
some very damaged supporting characters, Jenny

00:08:08.189 --> 00:08:13.840
and Captain Dan…things like that around
it. And so as perfect as Forrest might seem

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:22.390
and he is always sort of able to run through
all these situations, he has some very damaged

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:27.170
people around him that makes it so real.

00:08:27.170 --> 00:08:33.790
Erik: If you look at Lt. Dan or Jenny in Forrest
Gump, they are playing out their psychology,

00:08:33.790 --> 00:08:39.390
their family history, their wounds, their
problems, their worst tragedies that had happened

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:45.450
to them and they feel like it’s psychologically
real. And that’s what I mean when I say

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:50.070
real, it’s like psychologically based on
who they are. I think a writer of fiction

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:53.700
kind of has to kind of be a little bit of
a psychologist because you’re saying given

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:58.140
this person’s history and what they’ve
been through, how would they be now? How would

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:02.810
they react in this situation, how would they
go on pursuing what they want in their lives?

00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:09.700
And it’s going to vary based on where they
come from and what led them to this point.

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:16.730
So it’s not easy to do that really well
but when you do I think audiences respond

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:23.430
by totally buying in and being compelled by
this psychologically real feeling person that

00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:24.589
you’re putting on screen or on page.

